    {
      "id": "HEIKV1HNo9dEbCOr12A4s",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -1330.9094410491207,
      "y": -585.37457885236,
      "width": 1472.7360412338844,
      "height": 2128.1254663025634,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "bGz",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 269477339,
      "version": 1492,
      "versionNonce": 1166071573,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1743080689784,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. \nKafka first returns a metadata of the overview of brokers when producer\nconnects to the cluster. This config is maintained inside zookeeper.\n\nX = hash(\"A\") % number_of_partitions\n\nIf the topic has 2 partitions, the Xth partition is chosen\n\nUsing hash, its determined which broker will this request go to.\n\n{\n  \"brokers\": [\n    { \"broker_id\": 1, \"host\": \"broker1.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 },\n    { \"broker_id\": 2, \"host\": \"broker2.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 },\n    { \"broker_id\": 3, \"host\": \"broker3.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 }\n  ],\n  \"topics\": [\n    {\n      \"topic_name\": \"user_events\",\n      \"partitions\": [\n        {\n          \"partition_id\": 0,\n          \"leader_broker_id\": 2,\n          \"replicas\": [2, 3, 1],\n          \"isr\": [2, 3]\n        },\n        {\n          \"partition_id\": 1,\n          \"leader_broker_id\": 1,\n          \"replicas\": [1, 2, 3],\n          \"isr\": [1, 3]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"controller_broker_id\": 1\n}\n\n2. The brokers sends heart beats to zookeeper. If zookeeper stops receiving heart\nbeat from certain broker, it marks dead. \n\n3. Now, new leader needs to be elected by the controller broker who created the\n/controller node in zookeeper (although, all brokers continously monitors this node in\nzookeeper). The controller broker will elect a new leader for all the topics that had\nleader in the server/broker that went down.\n\n4. If the controller broker goes down, the controller node will disappear. Zookeeper\nallows another broker to create this node. Hence, making kafka fault tolerant.\n\n* A node is a DB entry in zookeeper",
      "fontSize": 34.050007460841016,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. \nKafka first returns a metadata of the overview of brokers when producer\nconnects to the cluster. This config is maintained inside zookeeper.\n\nX = hash(\"A\") % number_of_partitions\n\nIf the topic has 2 partitions, the Xth partition is chosen\n\nUsing hash, its determined which broker will this request go to.\n\n{\n  \"brokers\": [\n    { \"broker_id\": 1, \"host\": \"broker1.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 },\n    { \"broker_id\": 2, \"host\": \"broker2.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 },\n    { \"broker_id\": 3, \"host\": \"broker3.kafka-cluster.com\", \"port\": 9092 }\n  ],\n  \"topics\": [\n    {\n      \"topic_name\": \"user_events\",\n      \"partitions\": [\n        {\n          \"partition_id\": 0,\n          \"leader_broker_id\": 2,\n          \"replicas\": [2, 3, 1],\n          \"isr\": [2, 3]\n        },\n        {\n          \"partition_id\": 1,\n          \"leader_broker_id\": 1,\n          \"replicas\": [1, 2, 3],\n          \"isr\": [1, 3]\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"controller_broker_id\": 1\n}\n\n2. The brokers sends heart beats to zookeeper. If zookeeper stops receiving heart beat from certain broker, it marks dead. \n\n3. Now, new leader needs to be elected by the controller broker who created the /controller node in zookeeper (although, all brokers continously monitors this node in zookeeper). The controller broker will elect a new leader for all the topics that had leader in the server/broker that went down.\n\n4. If the controller broker goes down, the controller node will disappear. Zookeeper allows another broker to create this node. Hence, making kafka fault tolerant.\n\n* A node is a DB entry in zookeeper",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
